    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable that you are questioning this outcome. In a discipline like economics—which has well-documented gender imbalances at senior levels—an all-male hiring panel combined with a noticeable discrepancy in standard academic metrics (completed PhD, peer-reviewed publications, and university teaching experience) raises legitimate questions about process and bias.\n\nHere is an analysis of what an all-male panel might signify and how you can approach the situation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. What Might an All-Male Panel Signify?\n\n* **Potential Procedural or Policy Breach:** Most public universities have clear Equal Employment Opportunity (EEO) or Diversity, Equity, and Inclusion (DEI) guidelines governing search committees. Many institutions explicitly require gender diversity on hiring panels (or require approval from a dean/equity officer if a diverse panel cannot be formed). An all-male panel at a public university may indicate that standard institutional protocols were bypassed or ignored.\n* **Affinity Bias / \"Mini-Me\" Effect:** Research shows that homogeneous panels are more vulnerable to affinity bias and shifting criteria. When candidates are evaluated, subjective qualities like \"industry experience\" or \"departmental fit\" can be elevated over objective metrics (such as completed PhDs, publication count, and proven teaching record) to justify a preferred candidate.\n* **Reflective of Departmental Demographics:** In some cases, an all-male panel simply reflects the current senior faculty composition of the department. However, best practice at modern universities is to bring in an external female faculty member from a related discipline or an equity observer to avoid single-gender panels.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Alternative Factors to Consider\n\nBefore drawing definitive conclusions, keep in mind other factors that often influence academic hiring decisions:\n\n* **Departmental Strategic Needs:** The department may have had a specific accreditation, curricular, or funding priority that placed a premium on applied or corporate experience (e.g., setting up student internships, teaching executive education, or bridging industry partnerships).\n* **Interview and Job Talk Performance:** Search committees often weigh the job talk, teaching demonstration, and interaction with faculty/students heavily at the shortlist stage.\n* **Administrative Delays:** Two weeks without written feedback is not necessarily an indicator of evasion. Academic HR departments move slowly, and written feedback for shortlisted candidates often requires formal review by HR or legal counsel before release.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Recommended Next Steps\n\nIf you decide to pursue clarity, consider taking the following steps in order:\n\n#### A. Review Public Institutional Hiring Policies\nPublic universities typically publish their recruitment guidelines and committee composition rules online. Look up:\n* The university's **Academic Recruitment and Selection Policy** or **Staff Hiring Manual**.\n* Specific clauses regarding **selection committee composition** and **gender representation/diversity requirements**.\n\nIf the policy mandates a diverse panel, you have an objective, procedural basis to ask questions rather than having to prove subjective intent.\n\n#### B. Send a Professional Follow-Up for Feedback\nSend a polite, professional nudge regarding your request for feedback:\n> *\"I am following up on my request sent on [Date] regarding feedback on my interviews for the Lecturer in Economics position. As I continue my job search, constructive feedback on my application and interview performance would be very valuable for my professional development.\"*\n\n#### C. Consult a Confidential Campus Resource\nBefore raising concerns formally or publicly, consult:\n* **The University Ombudsperson:** An ombudsperson can offer confidential, informal guidance on university procedures and whether the search complied with internal rules.\n* **The Faculty Union / Academic Staff Association:** If you or colleagues belong to the academic union, their representatives are often very familiar with whether this department has a history of procedural hiring irregularities.\n\n#### D. Decide Whether to Raise Panel Composition\n* **If there was a policy violation:** You can raise this procedurally with the university’s HR/Equity office (e.g., *\"I noticed the panel did not reflect the university’s diversity requirements outlined in Section X of the recruitment policy\"*).\n* **If policies were followed (or guidelines were vague):** Pointing solely to the panel's gender composition without documented procedural breaches or written feedback is unlikely to change the outcome and could result in administrative pushback. It is usually best to secure written feedback first before deciding whether formal grievance or complaint is appropriate.\n",
